Problem: 219. Contains Duplicate II

Approach:
- Use a HashMap to store each element along with its latest index.
- While traversing the array, check if the current element already exists in the HashMap.
- If it exists, calculate the distance between the current index and the previously stored index.
- If the distance is less than or equal to k, return true.
- Otherwise, update the index of the current element in the HashMap.
- If no such pair is found, return false.

Time Complexity: O(n)
Reason: The array is traversed only once, and HashMap operations (containsKey, get, put) take O(1) on average.

Space Complexity: O(n)
Reason: In the worst case, all elements are unique, so the HashMap stores n elements.

code->

class Solution {
    public boolean containsNearbyDuplicate(int[] nums, int k) {
        HashMap <Integer,Integer> map=new HashMap<>();
        for(int i=0;i<nums.length;i++){
            int num=nums[i];
            if(map.containsKey(num)){
                if((i-map.get(num))<=k){
                    return true;
                }
            }
            map.put(nums[i],i);
    }
       return false; }
}
